{
  "requirement": "Validation MUST report a requirement or example with a missing closing fence or an empty body. Full names of `{req}` and `{example}` definitions MUST be unique across requirements, examples, and named definitions. Habeas MUST report every duplicate full name, including collisions involving named definitions, and identify both the duplicate declaration and the first declaration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.**\n\n**Implementation: `collect_definitions` in `src/evidence.rs`**, called by `load_definitions`, which turns any collected errors into an `InvalidData` error.\n- **Missing closing fence.** `definition_blocks` in `src/lib.rs` sets `closed = false` when no closing colon fence is found. `collect_definitions` then reports `\"{file}:{line}: {noun} {name} is missing a closing colon fence\"`.\n- **Empty body.** The body text is trimmed, and an empty result reports `\"{noun} {name} has no text\"`.\n- **Duplicates.** All `{req}`, `{example}` and `{definition}` blocks go into one `BTreeMap` keyed by full name. Requirements, examples and named definitions therefore share one namespace. A duplicate reports `\"{source}: duplicate {noun} {name}; first declared at {previous.source}\"`. That message names the duplicate's own location and the first declaration's location, and every duplicate is reported rather than just the first.\n\n**Tests**\n- **Unclosed requirement.** `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` checks the exact message with file and line.\n- **Duplicate requirement and empty requirement.** `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` checks `\"duplicate requirement requirements.one; first declared at md/requirements.md:3\"` and `\"requirement requirements.empty has no text\"`.\n- **Examples and cross-kind collisions.** `example_errors_include_duplicates_invalid_bodies_and_missing_tests` covers four cases:\n  - a duplicate requirement;\n  - an example colliding with a requirement of the same name, with the first-declared location in the message;\n  - a duplicate example and an empty example;\n  - an unclosed example.\n- **Named definitions.** `tests/definitions.rs`, in `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported`, covers a `{definition}` colliding with a `{req}` of the same name (`duplicate definition bad.x`). It also covers an unclosed `{definition}`.\n- **End-to-end.** `tests/authoring_batch.rs` shows the unclosed-fence error surfaces through the `author --all` CLI before any configuration or inference.\n\n**Minor gaps**\n- The `tests/definitions.rs` duplicate assertion checks only the prefix `duplicate definition bad.x`, not the \"first declared at\" text. The same message format is asserted in the other tests.\n- There is no explicit test for an empty named-definition body. The shared code path handles it, and the term parser would reject it anyway.\n\nNeither gap is material, and no FIXME markers apply.",
